00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.090
Nicole: OSINT or open-source intelligence
is information that comes from publicly

00:00:05.110 --> 00:00:09.190
available sources, that can be accessed
by the use of internet resources.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:14.050
These sources can be from television,
newspaper articles, research papers

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:18.180
and industry journals, census and
public record data, business and

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.459
trade documents, blogs and social
media posts, websites and more.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:26.500
Abraham: Some of this data can
be found just by a simple google

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:30.300
search, some search for much harder
to find information, that is not

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.800
found on normal search engines, and
these can include database, image,

00:00:35.039 --> 00:00:36.910
and deep and dark web searches.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.860
But as there is so much information
to go through, and so many different

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:42.620
sources, where do they start?

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:47.310
Nicole: When cyber security professionals
search for ‘open-source intelligence’

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:51.129
they are usually searching for
specific sources of information, and

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:55.830
often use advanced search functions,
specialist software tools and websites.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.100
Collecting and analysing data
gathered from these open sources

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:02.309
can produce actionable intelligence.

00:01:02.599 --> 00:01:06.910
It can be used for national security,
law enforcement, business intelligence

00:01:07.179 --> 00:01:10.700
and is of value to a wide range
of cyber security professionals,

00:01:10.869 --> 00:01:12.459
such as intelligence analysts.

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.590
Abraham: OSINT can be used for many
different things including ‘Counter

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:20.789
Terrorism’, ‘Violent Extremist Threat
Monitoring’, ‘Security Vetting’,

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:25.430
‘fraud and financial crime detection’,
‘organised crime and trafficking

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.700
detection’ and corporate security.

00:01:28.469 --> 00:01:31.990
So why is it important to only use
reliable and valid sources of Open

00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:33.650
Source Intelligence information?

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.420
Nicole: With so much Open Source
Intelligence information available,

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:41.229
cyber security professionals need
to make sure that their sources of

00:01:41.230 --> 00:01:43.820
information are both reliable and valid.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.180
Accessing and using unreliable sources
and inaccurate data can mean they

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:53.139
may be wasting time in searching
through information that is invalid,

00:01:53.390 --> 00:01:55.410
out of date or totally wrong.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:59.270
It may even involve chasing
false leads and missing relevant

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.850
vulnerabilities and threats.

00:02:01.799 --> 00:02:04.770
Abraham: Inaccurate sources of
information can also cause serious

00:02:04.779 --> 00:02:08.199
problems for organisations and
sometimes for other people.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.360
Imagine someone being falsely
identified or falsely accused.

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:16.269
This can lead to serious legal
issues, including large fines,

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.289
for breaches of the GDPR laws.

00:02:18.830 --> 00:02:23.059
Nicole: Although OSINT is readily and
publicly available, the use of it and its

00:02:23.129 --> 00:02:26.960
storage and processing is subject to GDPR
or General Data Protection Regulation, and

00:02:26.990 --> 00:02:32.480
any business or organisation using it must
adhere to the GDPR rules and regulations.

00:02:32.849 --> 00:02:37.310
This includes safeguarding that
information from cyber attack and having

00:02:37.310 --> 00:02:39.820
permission to process any personal data.

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.590
Abraham: One thing everyone should
think about is that data is considered

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:47.640
publicly available on social media, when
it is accessible not just to personal

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:50.030
contacts or friends but to everyone.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:55.390
SOCMINT or social media intelligence
is a form of OSINT that is often

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.660
used by companies and organisations
when hiring or firing employees for

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:01.200
something they have posted online.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:05.910
But remember threat actors also
have access to that data and

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:07.630
they don’t care about GDPR.

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:12.119
Nicole: So as we have said OSINT is
a valuable tool for cyber security

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.119
professionals and it stands to reason that
is also a valuable tool to threat actors.

00:03:17.559 --> 00:03:22.850
They can use OSINT tools and techniques to
search for and identify potential targets.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:26.690
This is why many small companies
and individuals, get hacked

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.340
because of all the OSINT, and the
vulnerabilities in their systems.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:35.179
Remember threat actors can track
online activities, as do social

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.510
media sites and they have all the
information on how users reach a site.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:44.580
Internet users need to be aware that their
research activity can be used to inform

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.380
others so should therefore be kept hidden.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.909
Abraham: OSINT in general can involve
a large amount of input from people

00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:54.299
to verify the validity of information.

00:03:55.139 --> 00:03:58.590
There is a potential for overload and
therefore the right tools are needed to

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:00.540
filter out the ‘noise’ or irrelevancies.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.050
However, relying on OSINT doesn’t remove
the need for traditional intelligence

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:09.250
gathering as they both should be used
in conjunction to get the best results.

00:04:10.119 --> 00:04:14.250
So, what happens if in general reliable
sources of information are not used?

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.200
Nicole: As we all know technology
is constantly changing and new

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.260
threats are also constantly emerging.

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:24.750
To make sure the intelligence about those
threats is reliable the source of the

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:26.929
information also needs to be reliable.

00:04:27.309 --> 00:04:31.010
Unreliable sources can often lead
to false information in the form of

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.450
false positives and false negatives.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:35.750
Abraham: What are false positives?

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:40.180
False positives are when a threat is
seen to be there, but it isn’t real.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.430
Investigating this can involve cyber
security teams in wasting many hours

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:48.810
and resources and may also distract from
seeing and following up a real threat.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:50.990
Nicole: What are false negatives?

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:55.560
False negatives lead cyber security
professionals to believe there is no

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:59.869
threat, when there is, or overlook a
threat that that may not seem dangerous.

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:03.489
This could reduce the ability
of the security team to respond

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:07.130
to or minimise an attack when it
happens as they are not prepared.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.969
A false negative could also be part of
hiding a more sophisticated attack which

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:16.430
is capable of evading early detection
therefore making it less preventable.

